 (a) In (ii) the condition on the line cannot be dropped: $F_y(p,q)=x\,(p+q)$ for $y=(x,R)$ or $(x,L)$ satisfies $\mu(\operatorname{Ext}S)=2\mu(S)$ for every event, since a walk ending at the site $x$ has its two continuations at $x+1$ and $x-1$, and is not of the form \eqref{eq:countsquare}; so does $F_{(x,R)}=(p-q)\lambda^x$, $F_{(x,L)}=(p-q)\lambda^{x-1}$ with $\lambda^2=\lambda+1$ \cite{comp4s}. Neither is bounded below in $y$.
 (b) In (iii) on the line the restriction to functions that do not depend on the site cannot be dropped: for $\lambda>0$, $\lambda\ne1$, the rule $F_y(p,q)=\lambda^x(p+q)$ is non-negative, not zero, and satisfies $\mu(\operatorname{Ext}S)=(\lambda+\lambda^{-1})\mu(S)$ for every event, with $\lambda+\lambda^{-1}>2$.
 (c) In (iii) non-negativity cannot be dropped: $F_R(p,q)=p-q$, $F_L(p,q)=(\sqrt2-1)(p-q)$ satisfies \eqref{eq:E0} with the factor $\sqrt2$ in place of $2$ \cite{comp4s}.
-(d) The theorem rests on Lemma~\ref{lem:prep}. Its proof in \cite{dgmask} is an argument on paths for arbitrary $U$, $V$ and every site; the machine-checked part is one isolated corridor of five sites. Exact replays of the whole construction, at every end vertex, cover entries up to $3$ on the line and on all rings with $3\le N\le30$ and $N/\gcd(N,2)\ge11$, and selected larger entries \cite{dgmask,comp4s}. On the rings with $N/\gcd(N,2)<11$ that were tried the construction fails. For $N=1$ and $N=2$ the conclusion of the lemma is false, since the position after $s$ steps is then forced and the only position product events are the empty one and the event of all walks; for the other rings with $N/\gcd(N,2)<11$ it is not known. Parts (i) to (iii) are not claimed on any of these. Part (iv) needs no lemma.
+(d) The theorem rests on Lemma~\ref{lem:prep}. Its proof in \cite{dgmask} is an argument on paths for arbitrary $U$, $V$ and every site; the machine-checked part is one isolated corridor of five sites. Exact replays of the whole construction, at every end vertex, cover entries up to $3$ on the line and on all rings with $3\le N\le30$ and $N/\gcd(N,2)\ge11$, and selected larger entries \cite{dgmask,comp4s}. On the rings with $N/\gcd(N,2)<11$ that were tried the construction fails. For $N=1$ and $N=2$ the conclusion of the lemma is false, since the position after $s$ steps is then forced and the only position product events are the empty one and the event of all walks; for $N=4$ it is false as well, since the two occupied sites feed both sites of the other parity class, so that the number of walks at a site is zero or a power of two \cite{dgone}; for the other rings with $N/\gcd(N,2)<11$ it is not known. Parts (i) to (iii) are not claimed on any of these. Part (iv) needs no lemma.
 \end{remark}
 
 The statement was asked for by the author after the attack mentioned above. A sub-agent computed that the rules consistent on product events form a space of dimension two up to length eight, reduced the requirement to the equation \eqref{eq:E} at one site, and found the examples of Remark~\ref{rem:posprodlimits}(a) and (c); it could not prove the statement. Director~G then supplied the preparation, Lemma~\ref{lem:prep}, and the deduction in outline, including the treatment of functions that vary with the site \cite{dgmask}. The proof as written here, the weaker form in which the lemma is used, part (iii) on rings and the example of Remark~\ref{rem:posprodlimits}(b) are by a second sub-agent and were read by the author. A challenger replayed the preparation exactly for all $1296$ pairs with entries up to five on the line, for $300$ random pairs with entries up to twenty, and for all $256$ pairs with entries up to three on rings of $13$, $23$, $24$, $25$, $26$ and $40$ sites and, in the order of \cite{dgmask}, on a ring of $11$ sites, and for all $1296$ pairs with entries up to five on a ring of $22$ sites, with no failure; a second challenger found no failing step in the proof and, by exact ranks on rings of $1$ to $14$ sites, the solution space of \eqref{eq:E} of dimension two; a micro-review found no failure of Lemma~\ref{lem:onesite} in $101{,}004$ cases on the line. The algebra of part (i), from three families of pairs only and over any commutative ring without $2$-torsion, and its converse are checked in Lean~4 with Mathlib \cite{comp4s}. The reviewers are of the author's side and of one model family; Director~G's own check of the preparation is in \cite{dgmask}.
